# Alloy Deposit Planner Implementation TODO
    Proposal: AlloyDepositPlannerProposal.md
    Scope: implement slot-bounded fallback search without changing ingredient calculations or inventory-access policy.
    Status: not started; the three-metal regression is intentionally failing.

    ## 1. Establish Regression Coverage
        [ ] Preserve Resolve_ThreeMetalAlloyWithOptionalForms_FindsFourSlotPlan as the primary failing regression.
            Keep targetUnits=100, normalized weights, requested counts 10/7/4, and approved supply quantities.
            Do not change rounding or relax assertions to make the test pass.
        [ ] Add a valid-concrete-layout test with newly available, smaller preferred forms.
            Assert execution-time resolution retains the exact existing layout.
        [ ] Add a fallback case where one form needs multiple slots because of its stack limit.
        [ ] Add a case requiring reduced use of a preferred form rather than simply skipping it.
        [ ] Add a case requiring backtracking to an earlier ingredient to leave room for later ingredients.
        [ ] Retain coverage for mandatory mixed forms and genuinely impossible arrangements.
        [ ] Add repeated-input assertions proving deterministic selection.
        [ ] Confirm new failures expose selection/layout defects rather than insufficient supply or invalid fixtures.

    ## 2. Prepare Shared Planning Data
        Depends on: 1
        Primary file: ../VanillaExpanded/src/AlloyCalculator/AlloyDepositStackPlanner.cs
        [ ] Extract existing merge-compatible grouping into a reusable private helper.
            Preserve smallest-source-stack order and stable ties.
            Aggregate by actual stack equality, not item code alone.
        [ ] Represent each ingredient's request, ordered forms, available counts, and effective capacities.
            Reuse existing types where practical; keep any new helper types private.
        [ ] Keep supplies and quantities detached from live inventory mutation.
        [ ] Preserve the service's existing CanHold eligibility and inventory-access checks.
        [ ] Handle nonpositive capacities as unusable forms without abandoning usable alternatives.
        [ ] Preserve the existing common cooking-slot capacity model.
        [ ] Run existing planner tests to detect accidental ordering or capacity changes.

    ## 3. Preserve Valid Concrete Layouts
        Depends on: 2
        [ ] Before greedy reselection, validate concrete targets when spreadAcrossCookingSlots is false.
        [ ] Validate unique and permitted destination indices.
        [ ] Validate positive quantities and each target's effective capacity.
        [ ] Validate desired forms against eligible supplies and the requested ingredient identity.
        [ ] Validate aggregate per-form supply and exact requested ingredient totals.
        [ ] Return the existing plan unchanged when valid.
        [ ] Fall through to selection when the layout is no longer feasible.
        [ ] Pass the layout-preservation regression and existing execution-layout tests.

    ## 4. Implement Slot-Bounded Fallback Search
        Depends on: 2, 3
        [ ] Retain the greedy selection as the preferred fast path.
            Preserve its output when feasible.
        [ ] Replace immediate rejection of an over-budget greedy selection with fallback search.
        [ ] Search in stable ingredient and form order.
        [ ] Track ingredient index, form index, remaining quantity, remaining slots, and selected contributions.
        [ ] Enumerate useful positive slot allocations in preference order, then skip the form.
            Try the greedy contribution first, then smaller allocations.
            Cap contribution by remaining request, available supply, and allocated capacity.
            Charge only slots actually needed; do not reserve empty slots.
        [ ] Backtrack when the current allocation prevents completion of later ingredients.
        [ ] Restore all branch-local selections on failure.
        [ ] Return the first complete feasible selection without altering ingredient totals.
        [ ] Return null only after all feasible branches have been exhausted.

    ## 5. Add Pruning and Failed-State Memoization
        Depends on: 4
        [ ] Prune when remaining supply cannot satisfy the current ingredient.
        [ ] Prune when an optimistic capacity bound cannot satisfy the request within the slot budget.
        [ ] Reserve a lower bound of one slot for every remaining nonzero ingredient.
        [ ] Memoize failed states by ingredient index, form index, remaining quantity, and remaining slots.
            Scope the cache to one supply snapshot.
            Document the assumption that ingredient supplies are disjoint.
        [ ] Do not add arbitrary search-attempt limits or success-shaped fallbacks.
        [ ] Re-run deterministic and cross-ingredient backtracking tests.

    ## 6. Materialize and Integrate
        Depends on: 4, 5
        [ ] Feed the selected forms into existing spare-slot balancing and target construction.
        [ ] Preserve one merge-compatible form per target.
        [ ] Ensure every target respects both item and cooking-slot capacity.
        [ ] Preserve exact ingredient totals and aggregate per-form availability.
        [ ] Keep execution-time supply recollection and permission revalidation intact.
        [ ] Keep ingredient shortages distinct from allocation failures.
        [ ] Keep planning free of transfers, packets, stack mutations, and fuel consumption.
        [ ] Keep deposit diagnostics Debug-only.

